> The tags are shown wrapped for readability; in the data each is a single
> `|`-separated string on one line. The object noun `ńrī` is the clearest
> derivation: lexical **HH**, realised **HM**, via
> `ToneCtrl=VerbForm:Part+NPPosition:Final` and `ToneMap=LowerRightmostToMid`.

## 3. Morpheme level

The verb `èrí` (VWORD) decomposes into a prefix and a root. The wrapper carries
the **realised word melody** (`Tone=LH`); the **derivation** lives on the
constituents.

| # | Morpheme | Lemma | Type | Tone (lex → real) | ATR |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3.1 | è | È | Prefix | Unsp → **L** | Minus |
| 3.2 | rí | rí | Root | H → **H** | Plus |

Full tags:

```
è     PART|PartType=Affix|MorphBoundary=Prefix|Aspect=Prog|Tone=L|
      Tone_Lemma=Unsp|Tone_Role=Dep|ToneCtrl=VerbRoot|ToneMap=Inverse|
      ATR=Minus|ATR_Role=Dep|ATRCtrl=VerbRoot+ClauseType:Main|ATRMap=Harmony|
      Context.Force=Declarative|Context.Polarity=Pos|Context.Focus=No

rí    ROOT|VerbType=Lex|VerbForm=Part|VerbClass=Dynamic|Tense=Pres|Aspect=Prog|
      Mood=Ind|Polarity=Pos|Tone=H|Tone_Lemma=H|Tone_Role=Dep|
      ToneCtrl=Tense:Pres+ClauseType:Main|ToneMap=Identity|ATR=Plus|
      ATRCtrl=VerbRoot|ATRMap=Identity|ClauseType=Main|
      Context.Force=Declarative|Context.Polarity=Pos|Context.Focus=No
```

The prefix `è-` is the ATR-dependent case: it has no fixed vowel of its own.
With the +ATR root *rí* it surfaces as **è-**; with a −ATR root such as *gbá*
the same prefix surfaces as **à-** (*à-gbá*). The root controls
(`ATRCtrl` on the prefix names `VerbRoot`); the affix copies.

## 4. Derivation, spelled out

Taking the object noun through the four-part model the framework uses:

```
underlying:  ńrí            (Tone_Lemma = HH)
trigger:     ToneCtrl = VerbForm:Part + NPPosition:Final
operation:   ToneMap  = LowerRightmostToMid
surface:     ńrī            (Tone = HM)
rule_id:     np_final_participle_lowering
```

Nothing is asserted: each step cites the environment and the operation, so the
surface form is reproducible from the lemma plus the rule.

## 5. Export

The same record exports to CoNLL-U at two levels — inter-token dependencies as a
standard treebank, and the morphemes of `èrí` as an intraword layer using the
multiword-token mechanism (the `3-3.2` range row). FEATS is abbreviated here for
width; the authoritative fields are the full tags above.

```conllu
# text = ọ́ nà èrí ńrī
# text_eng = He eats
1     ọ́    ó     PRON   _   Case=Nom|Tone=H|ATR=Minus                     0   root   _   _
2     nà    nà    AUX    _   Aspect=Prog|Tone=L|ATR=Minus                  3   aux    _   _
3-3.2 èrí   _     _      _   _                                             _   _      _   _
3     rí    rí    VERB   _   VerbForm=Part|Tone=H|ATR=Plus                 1   _      _   ToneMap=Identity
3.1   è     È     PART   _   Aspect=Prog|Tone=L|ATR=Minus|ATR_Role=Dep     3   _      _   ATRCtrl=VerbRoot
4     ńrī   ńrí   NOUN   _   Case=Acc|Tone=HM|Tone_Lemma=HH|ATR=Plus       3   obj    _   ToneMap=LowerRightmostToMid|Rule=np_final_participle_lowering
```

> This CoNLL-U is illustrative of the two-level structure and column usage;
> column values follow the mapping in the
> [annotation guide](annotation-guide.md#export-mapping). Replace with a real
> tool export before publishing.

## Why this matters

A conventional treebank would record that `ńrī` has tone HM and stop there. This
record additionally states **why** — the lexical HH, the triggering environment,
the operation, and a citable rule — so a single annotation pass yields a
treebank, a lexicon, a morpheme inventory, and executable derivation rules at
once.
